  "claims": [
    "Actual template substitution for proof tracks handles zero/one/two occurrences of an argument. The target step count equals template hole count times source step count.",
    "The existing sixteen argument squares extend to arbitrary eighteen-law tracks, including inverse proofs. The generated higher proof uses exactly one old local generator per source rewrite leaf. Boundary/path/shape/payload sizes are excluded from that count.",
    "An integer-valued output annotation is preserved by every old pure Higher.Generator and every old Four derivation, including all pure placements and separated exchanges.",
    "At an atomic identity, the left-unit and right-unit one-step reductions have annotation 1 and 0. Consequently no old pure higher comparison exists between these parallel paths.",
    "One explicit local unit-overlap family extends the pure presentation. It retains every old comparison and supplies the missing unit comparison. The extended root-family tag is Fin 19 and generator boundaries have at most three first-level rewrites.",
    "A generic mixed higher presentation retains the supplied old mixed basis and embeds the extended pure presentation. Old comparison support is unchanged; added pure cells have empty support; a circuit placement contributes exactly its own requirements.",
    "Rechecking the placement conditions yields correlated joint-law equality for the pure comparison endpoints inside one updated checked shared-state model."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "The obstruction is for the old pure Cartesian.Higher basis only. It is not a non-derivability theorem for the richer mixed basis, where other original higher generators may supply comparisons.",
    "The annotation is an algebraic invariant distinguishing proof paths. It is not runtime, encoded certificate size, guard support, semantic inequivalence of the underlying wires or a cryptographic advantage.",
    "Fin 19 classifies schematic families with arbitrary typed parameters and contextual closure. It does not assert a literally finite set of all instantiated cells or a complete homotopy basis.",
    "Only the displayed unit critical family is added. Associativity/unit and all remaining Cartesian/mixed root criticals have not been classified or resolved.",
    "Tensor-expansion argument naturality is available for whole tracks, but the canonical tensor fusion/wire-pass square remains open.",
    "The generic path-valued contextual contract has not yet been extended to the richer supplied presented basis.",
    "No fair advantage over ordinary proofs with identical sharing/lemmas is established. No new computational reduction, execution/time/key-generation theorem or MAC bound is derived.",
    "Novelty remains unconfirmed; independent review has not occurred."
  ],
  "primary_source_check": {
    "title": "Coherence in monoidal track categories",
    "authors": [
      "Yves Guiraud",
      "Philippe Malbos"
    ],
    "url": "https://webusers.imj-prg.fr/~yves.guiraud/articles/cohe.pdf",
    "arxiv": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.1055",
    "verified_scope": "Parsed text of Introduction, Theorem 1.2.3, the five-critical-branching paragraph in Proposition 2.3.3, and Corollary 2.3.4. This is not a full-paper reading claim.",
    "source_facts": "Theorem 1.2.3 assumes convergence and obtains a homotopy basis from generating confluences. The monoidal example in 2.3.3 studies five critical branchings.",
    "our_interpretation": "Critical-branching coherence is an existing method. The new countermodel identifies a concrete incompleteness of this implementation; it is not presented as a new general coherence principle. The source theorem is not applied without proving its assumptions for the actual language.",
    "visual_scope": "Web screenshot requests for page 14 of both public PDF copies failed with timeouts. No visual verification of the displayed critical diagrams is claimed."
  },
  "next_obligations": [
    "Classify actual root-root critical overlaps beyond wire-argument naturality. Resolve the remaining categorical and Cartesian families without arbitrary whole-path roots.",
    "Construct the tensor-internal comparison between component normalization followed by canonical tensor normalization and the actual whole-wire normalizer.",
    "Extend the path-valued contextual contract to the richer supplied higher basis and instantiate sequential fusion and the remaining actual roots.",
    "Preserve the original full research scope: finite complete mixed basis, computational reductions/losses/resources, continuing execution through oracle internals/keygen, fair total-cost comparison and theorem-level novelty."
  ],
